Date: 10 JAN 1980 1058-EST
From: H at MIT-AI (Jack Holloway)
To: (BUG SCHIP) at MIT-AI

First the good news.  The SCHEME chip was powered-up last night
and initial debugging has been started.  One of the packaged chips
has a gross defect in the VDD metalization around the CONTROL PLA,
as noticed by GJS.  The second chip tried has some problem that
shows up whenever it tries to do a nano-sequence.  No clear problem
has been located by optical inspection yet.  

The third chip shows many signs of life however!  It can single-step,
load and read state, and apparently run the garbage collector.  There
are some problems but they seem to be bugs in the interface that
we haven't tracked down yet.

Now the not so good news.  In spite of many simulations, both of the
microcode in LISP on the PDP-10 and also of the circuit, there is a
subtle bug in the GC microcode.  If a node being marked
points to itself in the CAR, the mark-node sequence follows the
CAR again right away.  This is because it tests to see if it is
going to trace in the CAR direction before actually setting the mark
bit for the current node.  The code is below:

(defpc mark-node
       (assign *leader* (&car (fetch *node-pointer*)))
       (cond ((and (&pointer? (fetch *leader*))
		   (not (&in-use? (fetch *leader*))))
	      (&mark-car-being-traced! (fetch *node-pointer*))
	      (&rplaca-and-mark! (fetch *node-pointer*) (fetch *stack-top*))
	      (go-to down-trace))
	     (t 
	      (&mark-in-use! (fetch *node-pointer*))
	      (go-to trace-cdr))))
 
H@MIT-AI 01/10/80 11:26:13 Re: SCHIP debugging
To: HIC at MIT-AI
CC: H at MIT-AI, GJS at MIT-AI, TK at MIT-AI
Got the chip to go thru a garbage collection finally.  There
are several bugs that I didn't get to trace down.

The fact that read-interrupt doesn't seem to work causes
the GC bug mentioned in the BUG-SCHIP note to happen fairly
regularly because NIL ends up pointing to NIL in the CAR.
To make the chip run around the read-interrupt bug, the following
works (?)
	(SCI-SETUP-MEMORY)	;This tries to clear 2000 SCHEME nodes.
	(SCI-DEBUG)
	L776
	S13			;should go to MARK+2
	D0 0 41 2		;fixup the read-interrupt
	S600			;start the sweep phase

At about step 2427 it finishes the GC and tries to run a program,
but seems to get an interrupt right away.  No interrupt is shown
on the status line, I hope it's not in the chip.

Also, when I had the contents of location 2 (CAR 1) set to 1000, the
garbage collector lost because the interface seemed to reference 66
when trying for 1066 (LISPM address).  I reduced the memtop to 200
so that it was below the 1000 boundary always.

There is some stuff to display register contents (type T when in debug
loop) but you better not do it when in a NANO sequence.  Unfortunately
this is hard to tell if you aren't following the chip step by step
and impossible to tell by a program.  The nano-run bit should have
come out of the package somehow, oh well.

Haven't tried #4 at all, this chip seems to work so far.
I will probably be gone until late this evening.  You might
look at the SCHSIM compiler if you run out of debug program hacking.

When we look at speed testing, I think we will do some sort of
multiple step run searching.  Start the chip at fast speed, run
N steps, record the state (STATE + some memory?), try again at
slow speed, and do binary search on the step count until the
first diverging point is detected.  This is essentially
what was done when testing CHEOPS in days of yore.
   
Date: 12 JAN 1980 0130-EST
From: H at MIT-AI (Jack Holloway)
Subject: Non-features
