The next time, it would be nice if NANO-RUN came out
externally, perhaps with the NANO-STATE when doing
a READ-STATE.  As it is, you can't stop in the
middle of a nano sequence and execute debug microcode.

The microcode for READ-INTERRUPT-VECTOR should only
assert CHIP-READ-INTERRUPT, it currently asserts READ
also....


Date: 12 JAN 1980 1612-EST
From: h at MIT-AI (Jack Holloway)
Sent-by: GJS at MIT-AI
Subject: Winnage
To: HIC at MIT-AI
CC: H at MIT-AI, GJS at MIT-AI, abell at PARC-MAXC

Played around for several hours, and finally got the
chip to reliably garbage-collect and execute simple
programs.

There is a logic bug in the god damn GC-NEEDED-PAD!!!!
We thought there would be a problem there, and there was,
in spite of 5 or 6 versions.  The Pad tests the ADDRESS-NEWCELL=BUS
at the beginning of PHI1, when the condition wasn't setup yet.
I used this to exercise the garbage collector interrupt stuff.
The GC was going off almost every other useful cycle in
the evaluator, but with a user GC handler function (it worked!)
the chip kept doing garbage collections until it got thru the
program. 

We'll have to patch this in the interface so that CHIP
GC-NEEDED isn't looked at, and use an invalid page to detect
when the CONSing has run out.  Then we can turn the page on
temporarily and give an external-interrupt request to the chip
pointing at the GC handler.  When the handler gets done,
it can reference the map itself and turn off access to that page.

The interface has a sight bug where single steps over 40 octal
screw the chip.  I haven't investigated.


 
