MSG: *MSG   71    
gjs, hic@MIT-AI 01/13/80 02:12:46 Re:  SCHEME-79 Lives!
	At approximately 0100 EST on 13 January 1980 the SCHEME-79
interpreter chip was declared alive and operational.  Gerald Jay Sussman
and Howard Cannon (midwife) were present.  The chip successfully executed
a recursive procedure using a Y operator for self-reference.  It was also
shown to successfully garbage collect and to accept external interrupt
requests.  The program which was executed is included below:

    (new-memory)

    (define (gcint . return)
      (mark)					;Do a GC
      (rplaca 7444002 377)				;Write interface map address to 377
      (rplacd 7444002 0)				;Mark it invalid so consing will stop
      (return))

    (define (null x)
    	(eq x nil))

    (define (y* f)
    	((lambda (g) (f (lambda () (g g))))
    	 (lambda (g) (f (lambda () (g g))))))

    (define (app l1 l2)
    	((y* (lambda (f)
    		(lambda (l1 l2)
    			(cond ((null l1) l2)
    			      (t (cons (car l1)
    				       ((f) (cdr l1) l2)))))))	;!
    	 l1
    	 l2))


    (&set-global-value (scheme-intern 'exp)
      (tcomp '(app ((lambda x x) 'a 'b 'c)
    	       ((lambda x x) 'd 'e 'f))
    	 nil))

    (&set-global-value (scheme-intern 'memtop)
    		   (pointer 'self-evaluating-immediate 200))

    The parents (gjs, h, gls, abell@parc) are now resting (on their
laurels).  A memo (MIT AI Memo 559) will be forthcoming.
   
